


:: start_lumira

I used to think I understood love. Little crushes in high school, fleeting warmth, momentary sparks   nothing that lasted, nothing that felt like it meant anything.

<span style="color: green; font-size: 30px;">Then came Ava.....</span>

What still surprises me is how quiet the beginning was. No lightning bolt, no dramatic moment. She just… slipped into my days like sunlight warming a room, slow and gentle until suddenly I couldn’t imagine the space without her.

We met in the campus café. Not a meet-cute, nothing memorable   at least, not at first. She always sat in the corner by the window, ribbon in her hair, hands wrapped around a mug like she needed its warmth to anchor her. I saw her before I <span style="color: green;">noticed</span> her. Then one afternoon, while I stared hopelessly at circuits homework, she peeked over her book.

<<ava "Are you… okay?">>

I laughed awkwardly.

<<leo "Just engineering trying to kill me.">>

She smiled   small, shy, but genuine. Something tugged inside me. The next day, we exchanged a wave. Two days later, a small conversation. A week later, I was sitting at her table without needing an excuse.

She was soft-spoken, thoughtful, the kind of person who moves carefully through the world without ever realizing how much space she fills in someone else’s mind.

One rainy afternoon, she came in drenched. I offered my spare hoodie before I could think twice. It swallowed her whole. She blushed.

<<ava "Thank you… You didn’t have to.">>

<<leo "I wanted to.">>

That was the first time I realized I liked her. Really liked her. What followed was a slow rhythm   study sessions, walks, quiet talks about fantasy novels, warm cups of tea, shared glances that lasted just a little too long.

She let me in carefully, like each small step forward was a gift. Her affection was gentle, deliberate. The first kiss was shy, the second, braver. She melted into me with every week that passed, even as her boundaries held firm. She wanted to wait. To go slow. To make intimacy something meaningful, not rushed.

I respected that. Even when desire grew sharp, even when my body ached for more, I waited because she was worth patience. Because loving Ava meant moving at the pace her heart felt safe and every time she whispered,

<<ava "Not yet…">>

I answered,

<<leo "That’s okay. I’m here.">>

A year in, I understood something simple and true, Ava loved slowly, but deeply and I was falling for her at exactly her pace.

<<set $day = 1>>
[[Let me tell you more of our story|start_lumira_2]]


:: start_lumira_2
There’s a moment in a relationship when everything shifts quietly no fireworks, no dramatic reveal just a soft realization that this person has woven themselves into your life without trying. With Ava, that moment came somewhere around half year...

We had fallen into an easy rhythm, shared café mornings, small walks between classes, evenings curled up watching movies. She wasn’t loud about her affection, she showed it in tiny gestures. A thumb brushing my palm. Her head leaning onto my shoulder. The way she’d whisper my name when she thought I wasn’t listening.

One autumn afternoon, we sat on a bench near the literature building, leaves scattering around us. She wasn’t reading, she was watching the wind move through the trees. Her voice was barely there when she said,

<<ava "This is my favorite kind of day.">>

<<leo "Why?">>

<<ava "Because everything slows down enough to breathe.">>

That line stayed with me. Ava saw the world softly, and somehow she made me want to see it that way too. Around that time, our closeness deepened. Kissing her became a language slow, warm, shy but full of something growing. 

She’d melt into me one moment, then pause the next, breath trembling, fingers curling at my shirt. Her boundaries were gentle but firm. When things heated too quickly, she’d whisper,

<<ava "Not yet…">>

Though the wanting inside me tightened, I always answered,

<<leo "Whenever you’re ready.">>

Because loving her wasn’t just wanting her body it was wanting her trust and she gave it in all these small, careful ways.

Over time, we built a life together in small rituals. Saturday café visits. Evening walks. Movie nights with her tucked against me, tracing tiny circles on my ribs when she got sleepy.

She wasn’t afraid of me she was afraid of rushing herself. Afraid of stepping over a line she wasn’t ready to approach yet. But she always came back to me, always curled into my arms afterward, trusting me with both her desire and her hesitation.

By One year, I knew I loved her deeply. Not suddenly, but through a thousand quiet moments. The way she bit her lip when she was nervous. The way she looked at me before every kiss, checking that I wanted her too. The way her boundaries weren’t walls they were steps she was building slowly.

I learned patience from her. Not the strained kind, but patience born of love. The kind that makes waiting feel like part of the journey. By the time a year passed, I wasn’t wondering whether she was the right person. I was wondering how I’d gotten so lucky to be the one she trusted.

<<set $day = 1>>
[[Back to the present|leo_dorm_room_lumira]]

:: day_1_1_lumira
<<set $day = 1>>

The vibration on my pillow is soft, familiar   the exact pattern Ava uses every morning. I’m barely awake, but I’m already smiling when I pick up.

Before I say anything, I hear her breath. She always exhales first. It’s like she’s gathering courage just to greet me.

<<ava "Um… good morning, Leo…">>

Her voice is warm with sleep, fragile around the edges. I picture her curled up the way she always is   blanket half-pulled over her face, hair a mess, phone clutched in both hands like she’s afraid it’ll slip away.

<<leo "Morning, angel. You sound cozy.">>

A shy sound, almost a hum.

<<ava "A little… I just… wanted to hear you.">>

That does something to me every time   this quiet little truth she drops like it’s nothing.

<<leo "You can wake me every morning if you want.">>

There’s a pause. I can feel her smiling through the line.

<<ava "Okay… I’d like that.">>

The day starts sweeter than the sunlight through my blinds.

<<link "Meet Ava for breakfast">><<goto day_1_2_lumira>><</link>>


:: day_1_2_lumira
She’s already at our spot when I walk in   tiny, tucked into the corner seat with her ribbon tied neatly and her tea cupped between her palms. A calmer picture of my morning than anything else in my life. She looks up, bright in that soft way only she can manage.

<<ava "Good morning… again.">>

I slide into the seat beside her because she always makes room, even when she pretends she isn’t doing it on purpose. Our knees brush. She pretends not to notice. I absolutely notice.

I break the top off my muffin the best part and set it on her napkin. She blinks like I’ve given her a present.

<<ava "You always do that…">>

<<leo "Because you like it. That’s reason enough.">>

Her cheeks warm pink. She takes the piece delicately, like she’s afraid she’ll drop it. The café hums around us   quiet students, the steam hiss of the espresso machine, forks tapping plates. 

Every time someone walks past too close, Ava tenses for a heartbeat, then pretends she didn’t. She sips her tea and watches me over the rim. I catch her. She flinches and looks away.

<<ava "Um… did you… sleep okay?">>

<<leo "Better now.">>

She blushes again. I don’t think she realizes how much she wears her feelings on her face.

<<link "Walk to campus together">><<goto day_1_3_lumira>><</link>>

:: day_1_3_lumira
Outside, the morning breeze is cool enough to make her drift closer. I don’t have to offer my hand   she slips hers into mine like it’s instinct, like that’s where it belongs. 

Her fingers are small and warm. They tighten every time someone brushes too close on the sidewalk.

<<ava "Your hand is… um… really warm today.">>

<<leo "Yours fits mine. Perfectly.">>

She tries not to react, but her shoulders relax a little.

We stop beneath the big oak near the campus gate   the first place she ever let me kiss her. The leaves cast flickering shapes across her face, and she looks up at me like she’s waiting, like she trusts me to set the pace.

She doesn’t have to ask. I lean in. The kiss starts gentle, just the way she likes it. Then she rises on her toes, meeting me more eagerly than she usually does. Her breath trembles against my mouth. My hand finds her waist, steadying her.

Then it slips. Just a little. Too high. Too quick. My fingers brush the curve of her breast through her shirt. She jolts like I burned her.

<<ava "Ah ">>

She steps back fast, eyes wide, breath shaking. She looks like she did something wrong, not me.

<<ava "I  I’m sorry  I didn’t  I wasn’t thinking  I didn’t mean ">>

Her hands twist together, pulling at her sleeves. She won’t look at me.

<<leo "Ava, hey  slow down. It was just an accident. You didn’t do anything wrong.">>

But she keeps shaking her head, cheeks flushed, panic rising in little waves I can practically feel.

<<ava "I shouldn’t have leaned so close  I made it weird  I’m really sorry ">>

Every apology hits me in the chest. This isn’t what I wanted. I didn’t even <span style="color: green;">mean</span> to touch her like that   not today, not like this. The moment breaks apart between us.

<<link "Head to class together">><<goto day_1_4_lumira>><</link>>


:: day_1_4_lumira
The walk to her lecture hall feels longer than usual. Ava keeps close, but not relaxed-close more like she’s afraid of leaving my side and afraid of staying there at the same time. 

She keeps glancing up at me, checking if I’m upset. I’m not, but I can’t seem to convince her of that. She stops just outside the classroom door, fidgeting with her ribbon.

<<ava "I… um… I’m sorry for earlier. I didn’t mean to ruin our morning.">>

I step closer, trying to catch her eyes.

<<leo "Ava, you didn’t ruin anything. Really. Look at me?">>

She tries   she really does   but her gaze flickers down again, too overwhelmed to hold it.

<<ava "I’ll… I’ll try to be better.">>

The words twist something in my stomach. Before I can tell her she doesn’t need to <span style="color: green;">be</span> anything but herself, she slips into the room, head lowered.

The door closes quietly behind her and I’m left standing in the hallway, wishing I understood how to stop her from apologizing for things she never needs to.

<<link "Check Messages">><<goto day_1_5_lumira>><</link>>


:: day_1_5_lumira
Campus looks normal, but everything inside me feels tilted after the morning. Ava’s last look before class keeps replaying in my head   the rounded shoulders, the forced smile, the way she whispered she’d “be better,” like she owed me something. She never owes me anything.

I keep checking my phone even though I know she won’t text yet. Ava never texts during class, she’s too careful, too rule-following   but habit has me opening my messages every ten minutes anyway. Finally, her name lights up my screen.

<<ava "Um… hi. I… just wanted to say sorry again.">>

My chest tightens.

<<leo "Ava, you don’t have to keep apologizing. I’m not upset." >>

The typing bubble appears… disappears… returns… goes away again. She’s overthinking every word in real time.

<<ava "I made things weird. I didn’t mean to. I hope you’re not... uncomfortable?" >>

Uncomfortable. That’s the last thing I feel. I’m terrified of hurting her, not the other way around.

<<leo "You didn’t make me uncomfortable. You’re okay. I just want you to feel safe with me." >>

Another long wait, then

<<ava "Okay… I’ll try.">>

Those two words hit hard. “I’ll try” is what she says when she’s scared but determined not to disappoint me.

<<link "Meet Ava during classes">><<goto day_1_6_lumira>><</link>>

:: day_1_6_lumira

On the way to my next class, I spot her across the quad   not close enough to talk, but close enough to see she’s moving carefully, like she’s afraid of taking up space. Before I can reach her, Jenna and Natalie intercept her. Jenna waves wildly.

<<jenna "Avaaa! There you are! We’ve been looking for you!" >>

Ava startles. Her smile flickers on thin, watery.

<<ava "Oh  hi… I’m okay, I was just heading to class.">>

Jenna’s grin fades fast.

<<jenna "Why do you sound like someone stole your lunch money?" >>

Natalie steps closer, voice soft.

<<natalie "You’re tense. Is everything alright?" >>

Ava hugs her books tighter.

<<ava "I’m fine. Really. Just… tired." >>

Jenna leans in, suspicious.

<<jenna "Uh-huh. Sure. I don’t buy that for a second." >>

Ava’s shoulders hunch.

<<ava "I have class soon. I should go." >>

Natalie watches her carefully.

<<natalie "If you need us… anything… we’re here." >>

Ava nods once, almost guilty, then slips past them and continues walking. I’m close enough now that Jenna sees me. She crosses her arms.

<<jenna "She’s upset about something. If someone said something to her " >>

Natalie touches her arm gently.

<<natalie "Let it be for now. She’ll talk when she can." >>

I glance after Ava, she’s already halfway down the path, walking quickly, eyes fixed on the ground. She didn’t even see me.

Later, between my own classes, I catch a glimpse of her again. Ava notices me, stops mid-step, and gives a tiny wave   a hopeful, nervous one, like she’s checking if I still want her here.

I wave back immediately. She smiles   the smallest one I’ve ever seen from her   then hurries off as if she’s afraid of bothering me by staying too long. God, I hate that she thinks like that.

<<link "Seek for Ava">><<goto day_1_7_lumira>><</link>>




:: day_1_7_lumira

My last class lets out early, and I spot Dylan and Marcus before I spot Ava. They’re loud   always loud   leaning against the brick wall near the quad, throwing a football lazily back and forth like they’re still in high school. I should’ve walked the other way.

<<dylan "Yo! Leo! Lover boy!">>

I roll my eyes, but they’re already jogging over.

<<marcus "Man, you look drained. What’s wrong? Too much cuddling with your girl?">>

I shove my hands in my pockets. I don’t want this. Not today.

<<leo "Just tired. Chill.">>

Dylan smirks, elbowing Marcus.

<<dylan "Tired from what? Bro, you’ve been dating her a whole decade and you’re still ">>

He drags out the last word with a grin.

<<dylan " *virgin mode activated*." >>

Marcus laughs way too loudly.

<<marcus "Man, if you don’t take your shot soon, someone else will." >>

My jaw tightens.

<<leo "Guys. Drop it." >>

But they never know when to shut up. They’re still laughing when I feel a shift in the air a quietness behind me that doesn’t belong in their noise.

I turn. Ava stands a few feet away. Frozen. Small. Eyes wide like she’s been hit. She must’ve heard everything.

Her fingers tighten around the strap of her bag. Her shoulders fold in. She looks like she’s shrinking into herself, trembling without moving. I step toward her.

<<leo "Ava, hey ">>

She shakes her head quickly, eyes stuck on the ground.

<<ava "I It’s fine. Don’t… don’t worry.">>

Her voice is tiny   thinner than I’ve ever heard it. She won’t even look at me. Dylan finally notices.

<<dylan "Oh oh shit, Ava hey, we were just messing around ">>

Marcus tries to speak too, but she flinches when he opens his mouth. She’s pale. Breathing shallow. Already backing up.

<<ava "I’m… sorry. I didn’t mean to… interrupt." >>

Interrupt. Like she wasn’t even allowed to exist in the space. I move closer, lowering my voice.

<<leo "Ava, you didn’t interrupt anything. They were being idiots.">>

But she’s already folding inward, tugging at her sleeves, apology written all over her face even though she’s the one hurting. She whispers so softly I barely catch it.

<<ava "I… should go.">>

She walks away before I can stop her   small steps, fast, like she’s running from the weight of those jokes crushing her chest. I glare at Dylan and Marcus, who suddenly look less amused.

<<leo "Great job, guys. Seriously." >>

I don’t wait for their excuses. I go after her.

<<link "Meet Ava at her dorm room">><<goto day_1_8_lumira>><</link>>



:: day_1_8_lumira

I catch up to Ava outside her dorm. She’s standing under the stairwell light, hands twisting together, chest rising too fast like she’s been holding her breath since the quad.

When she sees me, something in her collapses relief and fear tangled together. She steps into me without thinking, pressing her forehead against my chest.

<<ava "I’m sorry… I’m so sorry, Leo…">>

Her voice breaks against my shirt. I wrap my arms around her gently, guiding her inside her room. She clings tighter, like she’s afraid I might disappear if she lets go.

<<leo "Hey… you don’t have to be sorry. They were idiots. None of that was about you.">>

But she shakes her head, fingers curling into the back of my hoodie.

<<ava "They said those things because of me… because I’m slow… because I… can’t ">>

I cup the back of her head before she spirals.

<<leo "Ava. Stop. I don’t want anything you’re not ready for.">>

Her breath hitches. She looks up at me, eyes glossy. Then she rises on her toes and kisses me   hard. Too hard. Desperate. It’s not like her. Ava kisses like she lives, soft, cautious, thoughtful.

But tonight she’s pushing herself. Trying to quiet the hurt. Trying to erase the shame. Trying to prove something she shouldn’t have to prove.

Her hands slide up my chest, clutching at my shirt. Her mouth opens under mine, trembling. I pull back slightly to breathe, to slow her down She leans in again, urgent.

<<ava "Please… just… let me ">>

Her lips move against mine in a way that’s more panic than passion. I hold her shoulders gently.

<<leo "Ava… slow down. You don’t have to force anything.">>

The moment heat even threatens to rise between us, she jerks back like she’s touched a hot stove. Her whole body trembles.

<<ava "I I can’t  I’m sorry  I’m sorry  I’m so, so sorry ">>

She curls in on herself, arms wrapped tight around her ribcage. It hurts to watch   like she’s punishing herself for something she never owed me.

I sit with her on the edge of her bed. She leans against my shoulder, fragile and silent except for the occasional shaky breath.

For a long time, neither of us speaks. The tension sits between us   thick, heavy, unspoken   as if one wrong word might shatter her. Eventually she whispers,

<<ava "I didn't mean to ruin everything today…">>

I rest my hand over hers.

<<leo "You didn’t ruin anything. Not today. Not ever.">>

She nods, but I can tell she doesn’t believe it. Not yet. We lie down together, fully dressed, her head tucked under my chin, her breath still uneven.

She clings to me like she’s holding on for dear life. I hold her back, wishing I knew how to pull the guilt out of her chest with my hands.

The room stays dim and quiet. No kissing. No heat. Just two heartbeats pressed too close, and the distance somehow still growing.

<<link "Go to Dorm Room">><<goto leo_dorm_room_night_lumira>><</link>>



:: day_2_1_lumira

My phone rings before the sun’s even fully up. Ava never calls this early unless something’s wrong. I answer immediately.

<<leo "Ava? Hey  you okay?">>

There’s a shaky inhale on the other end.

<<ava "C-Can you… come over? Please?" >>

Her voice cracks halfway through the sentence. I’m already pulling on my hoodie.

<<leo "I’m on my way.">>

She doesn’t speak again, but I can hear the faint tremble in her breathing until the call disconnects. 

